# =============================================================================
# Local development environment
#
# This file is a template. Running `./dev.sh up` (or `./dev.sh render`)
# auto-generates `.env` from this file (replacing every [generated] marker
# with a unique random secret) and renders hapi-fhir/application-*.yaml
# from their .example templates.
#
# To create `.env` manually instead:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     # replace [generated] values with your own secrets
#
# To generate your own strong random secrets, any of these work:
#
#     od -An -N24 -tx1 /dev/urandom | tr -d ' \n'    # no extra tooling needed
#     openssl rand -hex 24
#     python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(24))'
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must never be committed.
# =============================================================================

# --- Secrets -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres superuser password (used by the postgres container on first boot)
POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD=[generated]

# Password for the `keycloak` database role
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=[generated]

# Bootstrap admin credentials for the Keycloak admin console
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=[generated]

# Password for the `hapi_fhir` database role
HAPI_FHIR_DB_PASSWORD=[generated]

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Hostname the containers use to reach Postgres. `postgres` is the service in
# docker-compose.yaml. To use an external instance, set this to its hostname or
# IP — or to host.docker.internal for one running on this machine — and
# provision the keycloak/hapi_fhir roles yourself (see the README).
POSTGRES_HOST=postgres

# Port Postgres listens on *inside* the network. Leave at 5432 unless an
# external instance listens elsewhere. This is NOT the host port; see
# POSTGRES_HOST_PORT under Host ports.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Keycloak ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The realm name and the application client id are fixed: `ohs-player` and
# `ohs-player-client`. They are written literally into
# keycloak/ohs-player-realm.json.example, docker-compose.yaml and the HAPI auth
# template, so there is no variable to keep in step with them.

# Canonical public base URL of Keycloak (scheme + host[:port], NO trailing
# slash, NO /realms path). This is the issuer authority: it is baked into the
# SPA, set as Keycloak's hostname, and used by the gateway to validate the
# token issuer — so it must be reachable BOTH from the browser AND from inside
# the gateway (and HAPI, in auth mode) containers. The realm path is appended
# automatically. See the proxy-mode block at the end of this file for the
# value to use when running behind the bundled nginx.
#
# `keycloak.localhost` satisfies both sides with one name: browsers resolve any
# *.localhost name to 127.0.0.1 with no /etc/hosts entry, and containers
# resolve it through Docker DNS via the keycloak service's fhir_net alias.
# `localhost` would not — inside the gateway container it is the gateway's own
# loopback. The port must stay equal to KEYCLOAK_PORT below, which Keycloak
# listens on inside the container and is published 1:1 to the host.
KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=http://keycloak.localhost:8081

# FHIR gateway admin client secret. The client id is fixed at fhir-gateway-admin;
# it is written literally into the realm and compose's IAM_PROVIDER_CLIENT_ID.
FHIR_GATEWAY_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=[generated]

# --- HAPI FHIR auth (only used when HAPI_CONFIG=application-auth.yaml) ------
# Client secret for the `hapi-fhir-server-client` Keycloak client
HAPI_FHIR_SERVER_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=[generated]

# Client secret for the `ohs-player-client` Keycloak client
OHS_PLAYER_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=[generated]

# Host and port of the application using `ohs-player-client` (no protocol).
# Must match the origin the browser uses — it populates the ohs-player-client
# redirect URIs and web origins in the realm.
OHS_PLAYER_APP_HOST=localhost:8084

# --- FHIR gateway access control ---------------------------------------------
# Access checkers registered in this gateway build (name -> source):
#   ohs_player_access  the ohs-player plugin's checker (default)
#   list, patient      ship with the gateway itself
# "permissive" disables access control entirely, and only takes effect when
# RUN_MODE=DEV — use that pair for local debugging only, never as a default.
# The gateway logs "List of registered access-checker factories" at startup;
# an unrecognised name makes every request return 500.
#
# RUN_MODE takes exactly two values: DEV or PROD. PROD is the enforcing mode
# and the correct default — access control stays on and the gateway refuses to
# load the "permissive" checker. DEV is the local-debugging mode and the only
# one in which "permissive" is accepted. Leave this at PROD unless you are
# deliberately bypassing access control.
ACCESS_CHECKER=ohs_player_access
RUN_MODE=PROD

# --- Build refs (git branch or tag to clone and build) -----------------------
# GATEWAY_REF  ohs-foundation/fhir-gateway
# PLUGIN_REF   ohs-foundation/ohs-player-reference-backend
# WEB_REF      ohs-foundation/ohs-player-reference-web-portal
GATEWAY_REF=main
PLUGIN_REF=main
WEB_REF=main

# --- Web app build args (baked into the SPA bundle at build time) ------------
# Browser-facing values. Changing any of these requires a rebuild:
# `./dev.sh up --web` re-runs the build. The OIDC issuer is derived from
# KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL + KEYCLOAK_REALM and needs no separate variable.
#
# VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL must be ABSOLUTE, and must name the origin the SPA itself
# is served from — not the gateway's. Both halves of that matter.
#
# Absolute, because the Portal derives the base for its /api/* endpoints by
# stripping a trailing `/fhir` from this value. Stripping `/fhir` from a bare
# `/fhir` leaves an empty string, and the Portal falls back to the original,
# so every custom endpoint is requested at /fhir/api/... . The gateway reads
# that as a FHIR resource named `api` and refuses it for want of a GET_API
# role that does not exist — a 403 that looks like a missing permission.
#
# The SPA's own origin, because nginx/spa.conf inside the web image forwards
# /fhir and /api/ to the gateway. Same-origin requests skip the CORS preflight,
# which the gateway plugin's /api/* endpoints cannot answer — they send no CORS
# headers. Naming the gateway directly here routes around spa.conf and
# reintroduces that failure. The gateway stays reachable on FHIR_GATEWAY_PORT
# for debugging.
#
# Keep the port in step with OHS_PLAYER_WEB_PORT below, and see the proxy block
# at the end of this file for the value to use with --proxy.
VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8084/fhir
VITE_FHIR_VERSION=R4

# --- Host ports --------------------------------------------------------------
# Change these if the defaults conflict with services already running on your
# machine.  Only the *host* side of the mapping is affected; containers still
# listen on their normal internal ports.
# Postgres defaults to 5433 to avoid conflicts with a local postgres install.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5433
# KEYCLOAK_PORT is the exception: Keycloak listens on it *inside* the container
# too (--http-port) and it is published 1:1, so host and container agree and
# KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL is one URL for browser and containers alike. Changing it
# means changing the port in KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL above and in the four
# Keycloak-facing proxy_pass lines in nginx/ohs-player.conf, which is not
# templated.
KEYCLOAK_PORT=8081
HAPI_FHIR_PORT=8082
FHIR_GATEWAY_PORT=8083
OHS_PLAYER_WEB_PORT=8084

# --- Analytics pipeline ports (profile: --pipes) -----------------------------
POSTGRES_ANALYTICS_PORT=5434
PIPELINE_PORT=8090
SUPERSET_PORT=8088

# Superset encryption key (required — set any long random string)
SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY=[generated]

# FHIR server the pipeline reads from. This is the transactional server, so the
# pipeline sees the same records the Portal writes. Reading is anonymous, which
# HAPI_CONFIG=application-auth.yaml does not allow — see SYNTH-STACK.md.
PIPELINE_FHIR_SOURCE=http://hapi-fhir:8080/fhir

# JVM heap for the pipeline controller (tune to ~50% of Docker memory limit).
# Quoted because `dev.sh` sources this file with bash: an unquoted value
# containing a space is parsed as an assignment followed by a command, which
# aborts the script. Quote any value you add that contains whitespace.
PIPELINE_JAVA_OPTS="-Xms512m -Xmx4g"

# --- Auth mode ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Controls which HAPI FHIR config file is mounted into the container
#   application-no-auth.yaml  — HAPI FHIR runs open, no token validation
#   application-auth.yaml     — HAPI FHIR validates tokens against Keycloak
#
# Switch modes by editing this value and re-running `./dev.sh up`
HAPI_CONFIG=application-no-auth.yaml

# --- Reverse proxy (profile: --proxy) ----------------------------------------
# Public hostname the bundled nginx answers on, and the host port it binds.
# Browsers resolve any *.localhost name to 127.0.0.1 without an /etc/hosts
# entry; for a different name, add one. Containers resolve this name through
# the nginx service's network alias, so no extra host mapping is needed.
PUBLIC_HOST=ohs-player.localhost
#
# PROXY_PORT is effectively fixed at 80. nginx listens on 80 inside the
# container and containers reach it through the PUBLIC_HOST alias on that
# internal port, while the browser uses the published port — so the two agree
# only at 80, and the port-less override values below are correct only then.
# Raising it means adding `:<PROXY_PORT>` to the browser-facing values while
# container-to-container traffic still goes to port 80, so the same variable
# can no longer serve both. Appending the port unconditionally would break the
# container side instead of fixing the browser side.
PROXY_PORT=80

# Running behind the proxy puts the SPA, the gateway and Keycloak on one
# origin. Two values above must change together to match — replace them with
# these, then re-run `./dev.sh up --proxy`:
#
#   KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=http://ohs-player.localhost
#   OHS_PLAYER_APP_HOST=ohs-player.localhost
#
# The first is baked into the SPA bundle and the second into the realm import,
# so `./dev.sh up --proxy` must re-render and rebuild after the edit.
#
# VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL must change with them, because it carries an origin:
#
#   VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL=http://ohs-player.localhost/fhir
#
# Switching an ALREADY-RUNNING stack also needs `./dev.sh reset` first —
# Keycloak imports the realm with IGNORE_EXISTING, so a re-rendered realm file
# is ignored once the realm is in the database. See the README's proxy section
# for what reset destroys.

# --- Public hostnames (server deployment) ------------------------------------
# One name per service. `./dev.sh render` writes each, along with its port above,
# into its own vhost under nginx/host/. Change a name here and re-render rather
# than editing the rendered configs. They are inert for a local stack, which
# addresses everything through localhost instead.
#
# All three must resolve publicly to the server's external IP.
KEYCLOAK_HOST=keycloak.example.org
GATEWAY_HOST=gateway.example.org
WEB_HOST=web.example.org

# The analytics pair, only meaningful with the --pipes profile
PIPELINE_HOST=
SUPERSET_HOST=

# Address the published container ports bind to. 0.0.0.0 locally so the stack is
# reachable from the browser and, if you want it, other machines on your network.
# Set it to 127.0.0.1 on a server, where host nginx should be the only public
# listener and nothing has any business reaching the containers around it.
# Postgres ignores this and is always on loopback.
BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0

# Contact address for the Let's Encrypt account. Set it and `./dev.sh nginx`
# runs certbot unattended, leave it empty and certbot prompts interactively.
CERTBOT_EMAIL=

# --- Server deployment -------------------------------------------------------
# On a server the containers sit behind the host's own nginx rather than the
# bundled --proxy one, with a subdomain per service and TLS terminated at the
# host. Set the names above, then bring the browser-facing values into line
# with them:
#
#   KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=https://keycloak.example.org
#   OHS_PLAYER_APP_HOST=web.example.org
#   VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL=https://web.example.org/fhir
#
# VITE_FHIR_BASE_URL names WEB_HOST, not GATEWAY_HOST. The web image's own nginx
# forwards /fhir and /api/ to the gateway over the compose network, so the
# browser only ever talks to WEB_HOST and no CORS is involved. Pointing it at
# GATEWAY_HOST works too, but only because gateway.conf.example adds the CORS
# headers the plugin's /api/* servlets do not send.
#
# The gateway subdomain is still worth publishing: the Client App and anything
# else native reads FHIR through it, and it is where curl goes when debugging.
#
# PIPELINE_HOST and SUPERSET_HOST need no matching value here. Nothing bakes
# their address into an image or a realm, so naming them is the whole job and
# `./dev.sh nginx` picks them up like the rest. Leave them empty unless you are
# running --pipes and want those two on the internet, and read the header of
# each vhost first, because the pipeline controller has no login of its own.
#
# Two more values make the stack itself server-shaped:
#
#   BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yaml:docker-compose.server.yml

# COMPOSE_FILE makes every docker compose call load the server overrides, so
# `./dev.sh up` picks them up with no extra flags. Those overrides set Keycloak's
# public issuer, give the gateway the extra_hosts entry it needs to reach
# Keycloak through nginx, and build the SPA against the public hostnames.
#
# Then install the vhosts and start the stack:
#
#   ./dev.sh nginx
#   ./dev.sh up
#
# `./dev.sh nginx` renders the vhosts, puts a temporary port 80 one in place for
# each name that still needs a certificate, runs certbot, then installs the real
# vhost. That order is the point. The rendered vhosts name certificate files, and
# nginx refuses to load a vhost whose certificate is missing, so installing them
# first would leave certbot unable to obtain the very certificate they need.
#
# One thing left to settle before this is a real deployment. The published ports
# are still bound to every interface, so the containers are reachable around
# nginx. Restrict them to loopback and leave nginx the only public listener. See
# the README's server section.
